  "comments": "<a href=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/5e73645e1093b.jpg\"><img src=\"https://en.numista.com/catalogue/images/miniatures/5e73645e1093b.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" /></a><br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\">The Royal Coat of arms</span> is different from the Coat of arms of Denmark and constitute as follows<br />\r\nThe first and fourth quarters represent Denmark by three crowned lions passant accompanied by nine hearts.<br />\r\nThe second quarter contains two lions passant representing Schleswig, a former Danish province now divided between Denmark and Germany.<br />\r\nThe third quarter contains a total of three symbols:-<br />\r\nTop: The Three Crowns represents the former Kalmar Union of Denmark, Norway &amp; Sweden (1397–1523)<br />\r\nBottom Left: Ram representing the Faroe Islands &amp; Bottom Right: Polar bear representing Greenland.<br /><br />\r\n<span style=\"text-decoration:underline;\">The Coat of arms of Denmark</span> is the Crowned heraldic shield of 3 lions &amp; 9 hearts (originally represented waterlily or sea leaves)",
